# Project workflow rules

## PRD drift detection
- This project uses a formal PRD at `PRD.md`.
- When you modify an implementation file, remind the user to run `/prd-sync` at session end.
- When the PRD file itself is modified, requirements may have changed — recommend `/prd-sync`.
- Never claim a requirement is implemented without a `file:line` reference.
- A test is not an implementation.
- If the implementation legitimately diverges from the PRD (obsolete or imprecise
  requirement), propose a PRD patch (`prd_patches`, review_state `proposed`) — never
  silently absorb the divergence, and never edit the PRD without user acceptance.

## Before solving any non-trivial problem
1. Search shared memory first via the `pattern-search` MCP tool (server-side hybrid
   semantic + keyword retrieval — pass text, get ranked results):
   - `query_text`: the full problem description
   - `project_id`: the PROJECT_ID from CLAUDE.md
   - `limit`: 8
2. Present the top results (problem, solution, tags, source project) and ask which to apply.
3. If you apply one or more results, record it with the `pattern-applied` tool
   (pass only the `_key`s actually used).

## After solving a reusable problem
- Offer to save it with the `save-pattern` MCP tool (it embeds the text and inserts the
  document with its embedding in one server-side step — required because the collection
  has a vector index; do NOT insert documents directly with `upsert-document`).
- Memory types: `pattern` (default), `feedback` (include `why` and `how_to_apply`),
  `user`, `project`, `reference`. Only save verified successes; never save secrets or
  client-specific data.

## Session end
- Remind the user to run `/prd-sync` if implementation files (or the PRD) changed.

> Note: Cursor does not run the Claude Code hooks in `.claude/` (session digest, drift
> queue, stop gate) — in Cursor these rules are the enforcement layer, so follow them.
